---
const notes = [
  { label: "01", text: "Started MTechZilla in 2021 to fix bad execution on good ideas." },
  { label: "02", text: "Decade across SaaS, IoT, and mobile before moving into AI systems." },
  { label: "03", text: "Now spend most hands-on time in retrieval, agents, and evals." },
];
---

<section id="about" class="py-20 md:py-28 border-b border-border">
  <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6">
    <div class="animate-on-scroll grid md:grid-cols-[220px_1fr] gap-10 md:gap-16">
      <div class="flex md:flex-col gap-4 md:gap-6 items-baseline md:items-start">
        <span class="index-numeral">01</span>
        <div>
          <div class="section-label mb-3">About Me</div>
          <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl font-extrabold leading-tight">
            AI practitioner.<br /><span class="text-primary">Still shipping code.</span>
          </h2>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-8">
        <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl leading-snug font-medium max-w-2xl">
          I started MTechZilla because too many good ideas were dying from bad execution.
          The highest-leverage work has since moved from "build the app" to
          <span class="text-primary">"build the AI system inside the app"</span> — so
          that's where I put my own hands now: retrieval pipelines, agent orchestration,
          evals, and the unglamorous guardrail work that keeps a model from embarrassing
          a client in production.
        </p>

        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-6 border-t border-border pt-6">
          {notes.map((note) => (
            <div class="flex flex-col gap-1.5">
              <span class="font-mono text-xs text-primary">{note.label}</span>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">{note.text}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
          I've built across the stack for over a decade, which is exactly why I don't
          treat AI features as a bolt-on demo — an agent still needs a real API contract,
          a cost budget, an eval suite, and a rollback plan. I also write regularly on
          freeCodeCamp; if I've solved a problem worth documenting, I'd rather share it
          than let it collect dust.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
